course: biology
lesson: 130
title: Ice Age: real woolly mammoths
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the film Ice Age, Manny the mammoth plods through the snow in shaggy fur, with small ears, a short tail and great curling tusks, and he looks like a hairy elephant.
explain: Manny is a fair picture: real woolly mammoths were close relatives of elephants, closest of all to the Asian elephant, about as tall as an African elephant, with two coats of hair and a fatty hump.
explain: Small ears and a short tail kept the cold out, because big ears lose heat; that is useful in hot Africa but dangerous on a frozen plain.
explain: We know all this because frozen ground in Siberia works like a freezer: baby mammoths have been found almost whole, one still with milk in her stomach, and Ice Age people painted mammoths on cave walls.
explain: The film gets the ending wrong: most mammoths died out about 10,000 years ago as the climate warmed and people hunted them, though a last small group lived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago.
fact: Woolly mammoths lived during the last Ice Age; their fur was up to about 90 centimetres long in places, with a woolly undercoat, long guard hairs and a thick layer of fat.
fact: Woolly mammoths had small ears and short tails, much smaller than an African elephant's, which kept heat in and were less likely to freeze.
fact: Mammoth tusks could be up to about 4 metres long and curved strongly upward and inward; they were used to sweep snow off grass, to fight and maybe to show off.
fact: Mammoth tusks have rings inside like tree rings, which scientists read to learn how the animal lived year by year.
fact: Frozen ground called permafrost works like a freezer; a baby mammoth called Lyuba, who died about 42,000 years ago, was found almost whole by a reindeer herder in 2007, with milk still in her stomach.
fact: Mammoths and early people lived at the same time; people hunted them, painted them in caves, and in parts of Ukraine built huts with mammoth bones as the frame.
fact: Most woolly mammoths died out about 10,000 years ago, but a small group lived on Wrangel Island in the Arctic Ocean until about 4,000 years ago, when the Egyptian pyramids were already standing.
fact: Mammoths were close relatives of today's elephants, closest of all to the Asian elephant, as scientists found by comparing DNA.
fact: Mammoths mostly ate grass and low plants of a cold, dry grassland called the mammoth steppe, perhaps over 150 kilograms a day, because grass gives little energy and a huge body in the cold needs a lot, ground by brick-sized ridged teeth.
fact: Woolly mammoths were about 3 metres tall at the shoulder, about as tall as an African elephant; the steppe mammoth and the Columbian mammoth could be over 4 metres.
fact: Mammoths had a fatty hump on their backs that stored energy for the hard winters.
fact: Ice Age people painted and carved mammoths in caves in France and Spain, and Rouffignac cave in France is called «the cave of a hundred mammoths»; the painters drew the hump and sloping back that frozen mammoths found much later really had, so they drew from life.
fact: Mammoth bones and teeth are dug up in many countries, including Britain, Germany, Ukraine and the United States, because mammoths roamed widely across the northern half of the world.
fact: Dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago, more than 60 million years before any mammoth lived.
fact: Some scientists hope to make cold-proof elephants with some mammoth features, but it has not been done, and many people question whether it should be.
exercise: How did woolly mammoths keep warm? | Two coats of hair and a thick layer of fat | A thin coat of feathers, like a giant bird of the snow | By living in caves all winter | By sleeping next to fires
exercise: Why did mammoths have small ears and short tails? | Small ears and tails keep heat in and are less likely to freeze | They were bitten off by wolves when the mammoths were young | To hear better in the snow | Because all the mammoths that were ever found were still babies
exercise: How long could a mammoth's tusks be? | Up to about 4 metres | Up to about 40 centimetres | Up to about 40 metres | Up to about 1 metre long
exercise: What did mammoths use their tusks for? | Sweeping snow off grass, fighting and showing off | Digging deep caves in the frozen hillsides to sleep in all winter | Catching fish through the ice | Climbing up steep mountains
exercise: How can scientists learn about a mammoth's life from its tusk? | Rings inside, like tree rings, record it year by year | By counting all the little scratches on the outside of the tusk | By weighing it on a scale | By smelling it carefully
exercise: Why have baby mammoths been found almost whole in Siberia? | Frozen ground called permafrost works like a freezer | People kept them carefully in museums for 40,000 years | They were turned to stone | They were wrapped in leaves by their mothers
exercise: What was still in Lyuba the baby mammoth's stomach? | Milk | Snow water | Stones | Fish bones
exercise: Did mammoths and people live at the same time? | Yes, people hunted them and painted them in caves | No, mammoths died out a long time before the first people | No, people came long after the last ice | Only in films like Ice Age
exercise: In parts of Ukraine, what did Ice Age people build with mammoth bones? | The frames of huts | Boats to cross rivers | Bridges over valleys | Fences round their fields
exercise: When did most woolly mammoths die out? | About 10,000 years ago | About 65 million years ago | Only about 100 years ago | Only about 1,000 years ago
exercise: Where did the last small group of mammoths live? | Wrangel Island in the Arctic Ocean | A hidden valley deep in the middle of Africa | The Amazon rainforest | The middle of the Sahara
exercise: Which living animal is the mammoth's closest relative? | The Asian elephant | The African rhino | The hippo | The walrus
exercise: What did mammoths mostly eat? | Grass and low plants of the cold mammoth steppe | Fish from frozen lakes | Meat from other animals | Leaves picked from the tall trees of the warm rainforest
exercise: How tall was a woolly mammoth at the shoulder? | About 3 metres, like an African elephant | About 10 metres, as tall as a house with three floors | About 1 metre, like a big dog | About 6 metres, like a giraffe
exercise: What was the fatty hump on a mammoth's back for? | Storing energy for hard winters | Storing water, like a camel's hump | Carrying its babies on long trips | Keeping its heavy tusks balanced when it walked
exercise: Where have Ice Age paintings of mammoths been found? | In caves in France and Spain | On the walls of Egyptian pyramids | On the walls of Roman temples | On old Chinese pottery and vases
exercise: Why are mammoth bones found in Britain, Ukraine and the United States? | Mammoths roamed widely across the northern half of the world | People carried the bones there from Siberia long ago to sell them at markets | They floated there on icebergs | Only one mammoth wandered very far
test: «Ice Age ends with mammoths still alive so they're still out there somewhere» What is right? | Most died out about 10,000 years ago and the last about 4,000 years ago | Right, a few of them still hide in the snowy forests of Siberia, far from people | Right, a few live in zoos | Right, they turned into elephants
test: «mammoths and dinosaurs fought each other, like in cartoons» What is right? | No, dinosaurs died out over 60 million years before mammoths lived | Yes, all the time | Yes, but only the biggest dinosaurs, which liked to hunt the baby mammoths | Yes, in the Ice Age
test: «2 mammoths + 3 elephants, how many trunks» How many? | 5 | 2 | 3 | 10
test: «Manny, Ellie and their baby, how many tusks if every grown-up's got 2 and the baby's got none» How many? | 4 | 6 | 2 | 3
test: «were the pyramids built before the last mammoth died» What is right? | Yes, the last mammoths were still alive when the pyramids were being built | No, mammoths died out millions of years before anybody built anything at all | No, pyramids are only 100 years old | No, mammoths lived after people left Egypt
test: What would happen to a mammoth with huge African-elephant ears in an Ice Age winter? | It would lose lots of heat and its ears might freeze | It would hear wolves better and stay warmer | Nothing at all would change | It would fly with its ears
test: «a mammoth's just an elephant in a fur coat lol» What is closest to the truth? | A close relative of the Asian elephant, with fur, fat, small ears and a hump | Yes, it is just a normal African elephant wearing a fur coat, nothing more than that | No, it is a kind of giant bear | No, it is related to the hippo
test: «they found a mammoth tooth, size of a brick, digging the new car park» What is right? | Mammoth teeth were big ridged grinders made for chewing tough grass | It must be a dinosaur egg, as those are about the size and shape of a brick | Mammoths had tiny teeth, so it is fake | It is a rock that looks like a tooth
test: «scientists are bringing back real mammoths next year» What is right? | Some hope to make cold-proof elephants with mammoth features, but it has not been done and many question it | True, it is already done, and the first baby mammoth will be in a zoo by next summer, the papers say | True, mammoths are already in zoos | False, no scientist has ever thought of it
test: A cave painting shows a mammoth with a hump and a sloping back. Why does this matter to scientists? | It matches frozen mammoths found later, so the painters drew from life | It proves that mammoths were pink and that the hump was only a painted shadow | It shows mammoths lived in France today | It shows mammoths had no tusks
test: «mammoth ate 150 kilos of plants a day, how is that even possible» Why did it need so much? | Grass has little goodness, and a huge body needs lots of energy in the cold | It was always greedy and kept on eating even when its tummy was completely full | It ate only snacks | It needed the plants to build nests
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the extinction of the woolly mammoth. | The greater part became extinct approximately ten thousand years ago, a remnant surviving upon Wrangel Island until approximately four thousand years ago | The species survives at the present time in remote and frozen regions of Siberia, unobserved by scientists | The species became extinct contemporaneously with the dinosaurs, upwards of sixty million years ago, and was never seen by any human being | The species became extinct approximately one hundred years ago
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the woolly mammoth possessed ears and a tail of small dimensions. | Small extremities lose less heat and are less susceptible to freezing | Small extremities permitted superior hearing of approaching wolves in deep snow | The extremities were habitually removed by predators | Small extremities were characteristic solely of juvenile animals
exam: Ascertain the agency by which the bodies of mammoths have been preserved nearly intact in Siberia. | Permanently frozen ground, designated permafrost, which arrests decay | The deliberate embalming of the bodies by the Ice Age peoples who hunted them | The conversion of the bodies into stone over a brief interval | The enclosure of the bodies within resin from trees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the woolly mammoth and living elephants? | The mammoth was a close relative of living elephants, nearest of all to the Asian elephant | The mammoth was a species of bear clothed in long fur | The mammoth was more closely related to the hippopotamus than to any elephant | The mammoth was identical in species to the African elephant
